\title{Anti-Gravity Theorems in Finite Dependency Spaces}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 21, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We introduce a graph-theoretic and topological framework that separates the cost of proving a theorem from its downstream influence. A finite theorem collection is equipped with a directed dependency relation and a natural-number proof-length function. The gravitational weight of a theorem is its number of direct users; an anti-gravity theorem has weight above a chosen minimum and proof length below a chosen maximum. Reversing dependency edges from foundations to users and taking reflexive transitive reachability produces a support preorder whose upward-closed subsets form an Alexandrov topology. We prove that a theorem class is dense in this topology if and only if every theorem supports a member of the class. Thus anti-gravity density is exactly dependency-cofinality. We also prove that a charging map into the anti-gravity class with fibers of size at most ten guarantees a ten-percent lower bound. An edgeless ten-theorem example shows that no unconditional ten-percent law is possible. Algorithms for classification, density testing, and charging verification are given, together with applications and limitations.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The difficulty of proving a theorem and the influence exerted by that theorem are distinct quantities. A technically demanding result may have few direct applications, while a concise lemma may be reused throughout an entire theory. Descriptions such as ``fundamental,'' ``deep,'' and ``elementary'' often blur these dimensions. This paper separates them by representing a body of mathematics as a finite directed dependency network.

Vertices represent theorems, arrows represent direct logical use, and a natural-number cost records proof length. The number of direct users measures local influence. We call a theorem \emph{anti-gravity} when this influence is high while proof cost is low, relative to stated thresholds.

The metaphor leads to an exact topology. Influence travels from a foundation toward its users, and repeated travel defines a support preorder. Upward-closed sets in this preorder are the open sets of an Alexandrov topology. The smallest neighborhood of a theorem is its full downstream dependency cone. In this topology, density has a precise interpretation: a class is dense exactly when every theorem supports a member of that class.

Density does not control cardinal proportion, so we develop a separate counting theorem. If every theorem can be charged to an anti-gravity theorem and no target receives more than ten charges, at least one tenth of the collection is anti-gravity. The hypothesis is a finite, auditable certificate rather than a universal empirical law. Indeed, ten isolated short theorems have no positive-weight member, disproving any unconditional ten-percent prediction.

\section{Finite theorem systems}

\begin{definition}[Finite theorem system]
A finite theorem system is a triple
\[
\cL=(V,\to,\ell),
\]
where $V$ is a finite set, $\to$ is a binary relation on $V$, and $\ell:V\to\N$ is a proof-length function. The expression $u\to x$ means that theorem $u$ directly uses theorem $x$; thus arrows point from users to foundations.
\end{definition}

No acyclicity assumption is needed. The cost $\ell(x)$ may count symbols, lines, inference steps, or weighted operations, provided that one convention is used consistently.

\begin{definition}[Gravitational weight]
The gravitational weight of $x\in V$ is its number of direct users:
\[
w(x)=\bigl|\{u\in V:u\to x\}\bigr|.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Anti-gravity theorem]
Fix a minimum weight $m\in\N$ and a maximum length $L\in\N$. A theorem $x\in V$ is anti-gravity at thresholds $(m,L)$ when
\[
w(x)\ge m \qquad\text{and}\qquad \ell(x)\le L.
\]
The anti-gravity class is
\[
\AG=\{x\in V:w(x)\ge m\text{ and }\ell(x)\le L\}.
\]
\end{definition}

The two inequalities are independent. A short theorem with no users is concise but not anti-gravity; a highly reused theorem with a large proof cost is influential but not lightweight.

\begin{lemma}[Threshold monotonicity]
If $m'\ge m$ and $L'\le L$, then $A_{m',L'}\subseteq A_{m,L}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $x\in A_{m',L'}$, one has $w(x)\ge m'\ge m$ and $\ell(x)\le L'\le L$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Support]
A theorem $x$ supports $y$, written $x\preceq y$, if there is a finite sequence
\[
x=x_0,x_1,\ldots,x_r=y
\]
such that $x_{i+1}\to x_i$ for $0\le i<r$. The case $r=0$ is allowed.
\end{definition}

Support is reflexive and transitive, hence a preorder. It need not be antisymmetric if the dependency relation contains cycles.

\begin{definition}[Dependency cone]
The dependency cone above $x$ is
\[
C(x)=\{y\in V:x\preceq y\}.
\]
It is the complete downstream region supported by $x$, including $x$ itself.
\end{definition}

\section{The dependency topology}

\begin{definition}[Dependency-open set]
A subset $U\subseteq V$ is dependency-open if it is upward closed under support:
\[
x\in U\text{ and }x\preceq y \quad\Longrightarrow\quad y\in U.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Alexandrov topology]
The dependency-open subsets form a topology $\tau_{\preceq}$ on $V$. Arbitrary intersections of dependency-open sets are also dependency-open, so this is an Alexandrov topology.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The sets $\varnothing$ and $V$ are upward closed. If two sets are upward closed, membership in their intersection propagates upward in both. If $x$ belongs to a union of upward-closed sets, it belongs to one member of the family, which contains every theorem above $x$; hence the union is upward closed. The same pointwise argument proves closure under arbitrary intersections.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Cone neighborhoods]
For every $x\in V$, the cone $C(x)$ is open, contains $x$, and is the smallest open set containing $x$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Reflexivity gives $x\in C(x)$. If $y\in C(x)$ and $y\preceq z$, transitivity gives $x\preceq z$, so $z\in C(x)$. If an open set $U$ contains $x$, upward closure forces every $y$ satisfying $x\preceq y$ into $U$; therefore $C(x)\subseteq U$.
\end{proof}

A subset $S\subseteq V$ is dense when every nonempty open set meets $S$, equivalently when its closure is all of $V$.

\begin{theorem}[Dependency-Density Criterion]\label{thm:density}
For every $S\subseteq V$, the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $S$ is dense in $(V,\tau_{\preceq})$;
\item for every $x\in V$, there is $y\in S$ such that $x\preceq y$.
\end{enumerate}
Thus a theorem class is dense exactly when every theorem supports a member of that class.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Assume $S$ is dense and fix $x$. The cone $C(x)$ is a nonempty open set, so it intersects $S$. Any $y\in C(x)\cap S$ satisfies $x\preceq y$.

Conversely, assume (ii), and let $U$ be a nonempty open set. Choose $x\in U$ and then $y\in S$ with $x\preceq y$. Upward closure gives $y\in U$, so $U\cap S$ is nonempty.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Anti-gravity density]\label{cor:agdense}
Fix $(m,L)$. The class $\AG$ is dense if and only if
\[
\forall x\in V\;\exists y\in V:
\quad x\preceq y,\quad w(y)\ge m,\quad \ell(y)\le L.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The displayed condition says exactly that every theorem supports a member of $\AG$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:density}.
\end{proof}

This criterion gives a concrete meaning to topological density: it is dependency-cofinality. It is directional, and it does not imply a large cardinal proportion. For example, if one theorem lies above every other theorem, its singleton can be dense.

\section{Quantitative abundance by charging}

\begin{definition}[Charging map]
A charging map into $\AG$ is a function $c:V\to\AG$. Its fiber over $a\in\AG$ is
\[
c^{-1}(a)=\{x\in V:c(x)=a\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Ten-Percent Charging Theorem]\label{thm:ten}
Suppose that a charging map $c:V\to\AG$ satisfies
\[
|c^{-1}(a)|\le 10
\]
for every $a\in\AG$. Then
\[
|V|\le 10|\AG|.
\]
Consequently $|\AG|\ge |V|/10$, or equivalently $|\AG|\ge\lceil |V|/10\rceil$ in integer form.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The fibers are disjoint and cover $V$. Therefore
\[
|V|=\sum_{a\in\AG}|c^{-1}(a)|
\le \sum_{a\in\AG}10=10|\AG|.
\]
The lower bounds follow by rearrangement.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[General bounded-fiber principle]
If $c:V\to\AG$ has every fiber of size at most $k\ge1$, then
\[
|V|\le k|\AG|,
\]
so the anti-gravity proportion is at least $1/k$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Repeat the fiber sum in Theorem~\ref{thm:ten} with $k$ in place of $10$.
\end{proof}

A charging map need not imply density because its target $c(x)$ might not be supported by $x$. Requiring compatibility with support joins the qualitative and quantitative conclusions.

\begin{proposition}[Support-respecting charging certificate]\label{prop:combined}
Suppose $c:V\to\AG$ satisfies $x\preceq c(x)$ for all $x\in V$ and $|c^{-1}(a)|\le k$ for every $a\in\AG$. Then $\AG$ is dense and
\[
|\AG|\ge |V|/k.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The first condition witnesses the cofinality in Corollary~\ref{cor:agdense}. The second gives the bounded-fiber inequality.
\end{proof}

\section{The obstruction to a universal percentage}

\begin{theorem}[Edgeless Ten-Theorem Counterexample]\label{thm:counterexample}
There is a theorem system with ten theorems, all of proof length $1$, in which every gravitational weight is $0$. At thresholds $(1,1)$, it has no anti-gravity theorem.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $V=\{0,1,\ldots,9\}$, let the dependency relation be empty, and set $\ell(x)=1$ for all $x$. Every direct-user set is empty, so $w(x)=0$ for every $x$. Membership in $A_{1,1}$ requires $w(x)\ge1$, which always fails. Thus $A_{1,1}=\varnothing$.
\end{proof}

The anti-gravity proportion in this example is zero. The same construction works for every positive minimum-weight threshold and every maximum-length threshold at least one. Proof brevity alone cannot create influence; any universal or empirical lower bound needs a structural assumption on dependencies, a different threshold regime, or a restriction on the class of systems.

In the edgeless example support is equality, so the dependency topology is discrete. The only dense subset is $V$ itself. Thus both the density and abundance failures are transparent.

\section{Algorithms}

Let $n=|V|$ and let $e$ be the number of dependency edges.

\subsection{Classification}
Scan the edge list once. For every edge $u\to x$, increment the weight counter of $x$. Then test $w(x)\ge m$ and $\ell(x)\le L$ for each vertex. With adjacency lists this requires $O(n+e)$ time and $O(n)$ auxiliary memory. An adjacency-matrix implementation takes $O(n^2)$ time.

\subsection{Density testing}
Theorem~\ref{thm:density} reduces density to reachability. Begin simultaneously from all vertices in $\AG$ and traverse dependency arrows toward foundations. Every visited theorem supports some anti-gravity theorem. The class is dense exactly when all vertices are visited. Multi-source breadth-first or depth-first search takes $O(n+e)$ time and $O(n)$ auxiliary memory.

\subsection{Charging verification and construction}
Given a proposed array $c(x)$, check that every target is in $\AG$ and count target frequencies. Capacity $k$ is respected exactly when the largest frequency is at most $k$. After classification, verification takes $O(n)$ time.

If arbitrary targets are allowed, a capacity-$k$ charging map exists exactly when $n\le k|\AG|$. For a support-respecting map, construct a bipartite graph connecting each theorem to the anti-gravity theorems in its cone, assign capacity $k$ to target vertices, and solve a maximum-flow problem. A flow of value $n$ yields the combined certificate of Proposition~\ref{prop:combined}.

\section{Examples and applications}

\begin{example}[Hub and spokes]
Let a foundation $f$ support two short intermediate results $p$ and $q$, each directly used by at least four later theorems. Then $p$ and $q$ are anti-gravity at threshold $m=4$ if their lengths meet the chosen bound. Nevertheless, the anti-gravity class need not be dense: a terminal theorem supports only itself and therefore has no anti-gravity theorem above it. High influence near the foundation does not automatically imply cofinality.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Terminal synthesis layer]
Suppose every theorem lies on a support path to a concise, heavily reused synthesis theorem. These synthesis theorems form a dense class. If no synthesis theorem represents more than ten starting theorems, a support-respecting charging certificate also proves the ten-percent bound.
\end{example}

The framework has several uses. In exposition, high-weight low-cost results are candidates for early presentation because they compress later arguments. In curriculum design, they identify modest prerequisites that unlock large downstream regions. In comparative proof organization, they reveal whether a presentation concentrates reuse in modular lemmas or duplicates arguments. The support preorder can also be supplemented with network measures such as transitive influence, discounted path counts, and bottleneck centrality.

\section{Limitations and discussion}

Direct-user weight is sensitive to presentation. Thin wrapper statements can redistribute or inflate direct uses without altering mathematical substance, while merging statements can suppress them. Proof length depends on notation, accepted background, and granularity. Thresholds must therefore be reported explicitly, and comparisons may require normalized costs or quantile-based thresholds.

Reachability records whether a path exists but ignores its length and multiplicity. A theorem reached through one long chain and another reached through many independent short chains belong equally to a cone. Refined influence measures could discount long paths or count independent routes.

Most importantly, density and abundance answer different questions. Density is an order-theoretic coverage property; bounded charging is a cardinality property. Neither implies the other without compatibility. Proposition~\ref{prop:combined} states one natural compatibility condition.

\section{Future directions}

Empirical work can extract dependency graphs and proof-cost measurements from substantial mathematical collections, evaluate threshold sensitivity, and search for bounded charging certificates. Direct-user weight should be compared with transitive downstream counts, discounted paths, and centrality. Robustness under refactoring deserves particular attention: wrapper insertion and theorem splitting should either be quotiented out or penalized.

A structural theory could seek hypotheses on growing dependency networks that force cofinality of concise, high-weight vertices. For sequences of expanding systems, fixed proportions can be replaced by lower asymptotic density. The topology itself extends beyond finite systems: upward-closed subsets of any preorder form an Alexandrov topology, and Theorem~\ref{thm:density} remains valid. Finiteness is needed here for cardinal weights, effective proportions, and bounded-fiber counting.

\section{Conclusion}

Gravitational weight records direct reuse, while anti-gravity selects theorems that combine influence with concise proof. Support reachability transforms dependency data into an Alexandrov space. In that space, density is exactly dependency-cofinality. Quantitative prevalence requires an additional certificate: fibers bounded by ten imply a ten-percent lower bound, and support-respecting fibers certify density and prevalence simultaneously.

The edgeless ten-theorem construction shows why the hypotheses matter. Short proofs alone do not produce influence, and no positive universal percentage survives without dependency structure. The framework therefore separates three claims that should not be conflated: a topological equivalence, a conditional counting theorem, and an unconditional counterexample.

