# The Architecture of Mathematical Influence

A theorem can be short yet support a vast downstream theory. This notebook explores that separation between **proof cost** and **reuse**.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The first widget applies the definition directly: a theorem is anti-gravity at thresholds $(m,L)$ when its direct-user weight is at least $m$ and its proof length is at most $L$. See the [Alexandrov topology overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexandrov_topology) for background on the topology used below.

{{visualization:0}}

## From edges to open sets

Support follows paths from foundations to users. A dependency-open set contains everything downstream of each member.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the density proof</summary>

For a theorem $x$, its cone $C(x)$ consists of all theorems it supports. This cone is open and contains $x$. If a class $S$ is dense, $C(x)$ meets $S$, so $x$ supports a member of $S$. Conversely, if every $x$ supports some $y\in S$, every nonempty open set containing $x$ must also contain $y$, and therefore meets $S$.

</details>

{{algorithm:1}}

## From density to percentages

Density is qualitative, so a charging certificate supplies the count. Assign every theorem to an anti-gravity theorem, with no target receiving more than $k$ assignments. Then $|V|\le k|A|$.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the ten-percent counting argument</summary>

The fibers of the charging map partition $V$. If each has size at most $10$, then $|V|=\sum_{a\in A}|c^{-1}(a)|\le10|A|$. Thus $|A|\ge |V|/10$.

</details>

{{visualization:1}}

## Boundary cases and reproducible examples

The edgeless ten-theorem system has weight zero everywhere, despite every proof having length one. It refutes an unconditional percentage law and shows why dependency assumptions matter.

{{demo:0}}

{{demo:1}}

{{demo:2}}

{{algorithm:0}}

For broader graph-theoretic context, consult resources on [directed graphs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Directed_graph), [reachability](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reachability), and the [pigeonhole principle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pigeonhole_principle).

